  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation.",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}))(x-(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i})-x(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})+(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x\\sqrt{3}-x\\text{i}-x\\sqrt{3}+x\\text{i}+3+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+3+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\sqrt{3}\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+4) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
